---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../../components/RelatedTools.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { name: 'Nutrition & Body Composition', href: '/tools/nutrition/' },
  { name: 'BMR Calculator' },
];

/*
 * FAQ answers are passed to Base.astro's `faqs` prop.
 * HTML <a> tags inside answers render on-page via set:html and also flow into
 * the FAQPage JSON-LD schema. Per Google's structured data documentation,
 * <a> tags are explicitly allowed inside acceptedAnswer.text. Bing also
 * supports FAQ rich results with HTML links. Keep links limited to internal
 * tools and authoritative sources — no inline styles, no promotional copy.
 */
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?',
    answer:
      'BMR estimates the energy your body uses at complete rest. TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) estimates your total daily energy needs after adding movement, exercise, and digestion. BMR is typically a large share of TDEE — often cited as 60–75% for most adults — but the exact share varies with activity level. Use our <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/">TDEE Calculator</a> for a complete daily calorie estimate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between BMR and RMR?',
    answer:
      'BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) and RMR (Resting Metabolic Rate) both describe resting energy expenditure, but BMR requires stricter measurement conditions. In practice, RMR tends to be slightly higher (about 5–10%) and is what most predictive equations like Mifflin-St Jeor actually estimate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which BMR formula is more accurate — Mifflin-St Jeor or Harris-Benedict?',
    answer:
      'The Mifflin-St Jeor equation (1990) is generally considered the more accurate predictive formula for modern adults. The Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics has endorsed it as the preferred equation for clinical use. In the original Mifflin-St Jeor comparison research, the older Harris-Benedict equation tended to overestimate resting energy needs by roughly 5%.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a good BMR?',
    answer:
      'There is no single BMR value that is good or bad for everyone. BMR varies with body size, height, weight, age, sex, and body composition. Typical adult BMRs range from about 1,200–1,500 kcal/day for women and 1,500–1,900 kcal/day for men, but individual variation is wide. A calculator estimate should be interpreted in context, not as a health score.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is a BMR of 1400 good or bad?',
    answer:
      'A BMR of 1400 kcal/day is a common resting energy value for an average-sized adult female. It simply means the body burns about 1400 calories per day just staying alive at rest. It is not inherently healthy or unhealthy — BMR alone is not a health rating.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How accurate are online BMR calculators?',
    answer:
      'Validation studies often show predictive BMR equations accurate within about 10% for average-weight, healthy adults. Accuracy tends to be lower for very muscular individuals, people with obesity, and some clinical populations. If you need a precise measurement, resting energy expenditure can be measured with indirect calorimetry in a clinical or research setting.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I eat my BMR or my TDEE to lose weight?',
    answer:
      'Neither by default. Eating at BMR is usually too low for most adults because BMR excludes daily activity. A more practical approach is to estimate your <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/">TDEE</a>, choose a moderate deficit, and then track real-world progress over several weeks. Use the <a href="/tools/nutrition/calorie-deficit-calculator/">Calorie Deficit Calculator</a> for a target that fits your goal.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use BMR to calculate calories for weight loss?',
    answer:
      'BMR alone does not represent your total daily calorie needs. A more useful approach is to estimate your TDEE and use that as a starting point. Then use the <a href="/tools/nutrition/calorie-deficit-calculator/">Calorie Deficit Calculator</a> to plan a daily target and timeline.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How often should I recalculate my BMR?',
    answer:
      'Recalculate BMR whenever your body weight changes by about 10 lb (4.5 kg), your age changes, or your body composition shifts significantly. Predictive equations depend on weight, height, age, and sex, so meaningful changes in any of those affect the estimate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I increase my BMR naturally?',
    answer:
      'BMR is largely determined by body size and composition. The most meaningful lever is increasing lean muscle mass through resistance training, though the effect on total daily calorie needs is usually smaller than commonly claimed. Adequate protein intake, sleep, and overall health also support a normal BMR.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does building muscle increase BMR?',
    answer:
      'Yes, muscle tissue is metabolically active. However, the effect of gaining muscle on total daily energy needs is often overstated online. Estimates for the resting energy cost of muscle tissue vary widely across studies — commonly cited values are around 6–10 calories per pound per day, so a realistic 5–10 lb (2.3–4.5 kg) muscle gain might add roughly 30–100 kcal/day to BMR.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why can my BMR change when I lose weight?',
    answer:
      'Your estimated resting energy needs decrease as body weight decreases, because a smaller body has less tissue mass to maintain at rest. Beyond that, some people experience a slightly larger drop known as adaptive thermogenesis, where energy expenditure falls a bit more than predicted by weight loss alone.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can BMR be measured instead of calculated?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Resting energy expenditure can be measured using indirect calorimetry under controlled conditions in clinical or research settings. Online BMR calculators provide predictive estimates from variables such as age, sex, height, and weight.',
  },
];

const appSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  '@id': 'https://repmaths.com/tools/nutrition/bmr-calculator/#calculator',
  name: 'BMR Calculator (Mifflin-St Jeor & Harris-Benedict)',
  alternateName: 'Basal Metabolic Rate Calculator',
  url: 'https://repmaths.com/tools/nutrition/bmr-calculator/',
  description:
    'Free BMR calculator using the Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict equations. Estimate basal metabolic rate in lb or kg and compare BMR vs TDEE and RMR.',
  applicationCategory: 'HealthApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Any',
  browserRequirements: 'Requires JavaScript',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  featureList:
    'Mifflin-St Jeor equation, Harris-Benedict equation, lb and kg input, ft-in and cm input, BMR to TDEE conversion, BMR vs RMR comparison, kJ/day output',
  offers: {
    '@type': 'Offer',
    price: '0',
    priceCurrency: 'USD',
  },
  publisher: {
    '@id': 'https://repmaths.com/#organization',
  },
  isPartOf: {
    '@id': 'https://repmaths.com/#website',
  },
};

const jsonLdSafe = (value: unknown) =>
  JSON.stringify(value).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');
---

<Base
  title="BMR Calculator — Mifflin-St Jeor & Harris-Benedict"
  description="Free BMR calculator for men & women. Calculate basal metabolic rate using Mifflin-St Jeor or Harris-Benedict in lb, ft/in, or kg, cm. Compare BMR vs TDEE vs RMR."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={jsonLdSafe(appSchema)}
  />

  <div class="tool-page">
    <header class="tool-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Nutrition &amp; Body Composition</p>
      <h1>BMR Calculator (Mifflin-St Jeor &amp; Harris-Benedict)</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        Estimate your Basal Metabolic Rate — the energy your body uses at
        complete rest — using the Mifflin-St Jeor or revised Harris-Benedict
        equation. Results update automatically as you enter your details.
      </p>
    </header>

    <aside class="quick-answer" aria-label="BMR quick answer">
      <p>
        <strong>What is BMR?</strong> BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) is the energy
        your body uses at rest for essential functions like breathing and
        circulation. A 175 lb (79.4 kg), 5&prime;10&Prime; (177.8 cm),
        30-year-old male has a BMR of roughly <strong>1,760 kcal/day</strong>
        using Mifflin-St Jeor. Formula:
        <code>Men: 10×kg + 6.25×cm − 5×age + 5</code>. Use our
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/">TDEE Calculator</a> for
        total daily calorie needs.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <section class="calculator-card" aria-labelledby="calculator-heading">
      <h2 id="calculator-heading">Calculate Your BMR</h2>

      <div class="form-grid">
        <div class="field">
          <label>Unit system</label>
          <div class="unit-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Unit system">
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn active" data-unit="imperial" aria-pressed="true">Imperial (lb, ft-in)</button>
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn" data-unit="metric" aria-pressed="false">Metric (kg, cm)</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label>Formula</label>
          <div class="unit-toggle" role="group" aria-label="BMR formula">
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn active" data-formula="mifflin" aria-pressed="true">Mifflin-St Jeor</button>
            <button type="button" class="unit-btn" data-formula="harris" aria-pressed="false">Harris-Benedict</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label for="sex">Sex</label>
          <select id="sex" aria-describedby="error">
            <option value="male">Male</option>
            <option value="female">Female</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label for="age">Age</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="age" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 30" inputmode="numeric" min="18" max="90" step="1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span>years</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Imperial inputs -->
        <div class="field" id="weight-lb-field">
          <label for="weight-lb" id="weight-lb-label">Weight (lb)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="weight-lb" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 175" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span>lb</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field" id="height-imperial-field">
          <span class="field-label">Height (ft / in)</span>
          <div class="height-row">
            <div class="input-with-unit">
              <label for="height-ft" class="sr-only">Height (feet)</label>
              <input id="height-ft" type="number" placeholder="ft" inputmode="numeric" min="4" max="8" step="1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
              <span>ft</span>
            </div>
            <div class="input-with-unit">
              <label for="height-in" class="sr-only">Height (inches)</label>
              <input id="height-in" type="number" placeholder="in" inputmode="decimal" min="0" max="11.9" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
              <span>in</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Metric inputs -->
        <div class="field" id="weight-kg-field" hidden>
          <label for="weight-kg" id="weight-kg-label">Weight (kg)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="weight-kg" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 79.4" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span>kg</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="field" id="height-cm-field" hidden>
          <label for="height-cm" id="height-cm-label">Height (cm)</label>
          <div class="input-with-unit">
            <input id="height-cm" type="number" placeholder="e.g. 178" inputmode="decimal" step="0.1" autocomplete="off" aria-describedby="error" />
            <span>cm</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p id="error" class="message error" role="alert" hidden></p>

      <div id="results" class="results">
        <div
          id="result-announcement"
          class="sr-only"
          role="status"
          aria-live="polite"
          aria-atomic="true"
        ></div>

        <div class="result-primary">
          <span class="result-label">Estimated BMR</span>
          <strong id="result-bmr">—</strong>
          <span class="result-unit">kcal/day</span>
          <span class="result-secondary" id="result-kj">— kJ/day</span>
        </div>

        <!-- TDEE preview: BMR × activity factor -->
        <div class="tdee-preview" id="tdee-preview" hidden>
          <h3>Estimated TDEE by Activity Level</h3>
          <p class="tdee-preview-sub">
            Resting energy expenditure typically accounts for a substantial
            share of daily energy needs, though the exact proportion varies
            with activity level. Here is what your estimated total daily
            expenditure would look like at three common activity levels:
          </p>
          <div class="tdee-preview-table">
            <div>
              <span>Sedentary</span>
              <strong id="tdee-sedentary">—</strong>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span>Lightly active</span>
              <strong id="tdee-light">—</strong>
            </div>
            <div>
              <span>Moderately active</span>
              <strong id="tdee-moderate">—</strong>
            </div>
          </div>
          <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/" class="tdee-preview-cta">
            Get your personalized TDEE →
          </a>
        </div>

        <p id="warning" class="message warning" hidden></p>

        <div class="result-actions">
          <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/" class="btn-primary">
            Calculate Your TDEE →
          </a>
          <a href="/tools/nutrition/calorie-deficit-calculator/" class="btn-secondary">
            Plan a calorie deficit
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="calculator-note">
        This calculator provides estimates for adults ages 18–90 and is not
        medical advice. Predictive equations can differ from measured resting
        energy expenditure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>What Is Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)?</h2>

      <p>
        Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) is an estimate of how much energy your body
        uses at complete rest to support essential functions — breathing,
        circulation, cell production, and maintaining normal body temperature.
      </p>

      <p>
        BMR is only one part of your total daily energy needs. Movement,
        exercise, digestion, and other activities require additional energy on
        top of BMR. That is why BMR should never be treated as your
        maintenance calorie target.
      </p>

      <p>
        This calculator estimates BMR using two widely used predictive
        equations: the
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mifflin-St Jeor equation</a>
        (1990) and the
        <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6741850/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">revised Harris-Benedict equation</a>
        (Roza &amp; Shizgal, 1984).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>How RepMaths Calculates Your BMR</h2>

      <p>
        This calculator is a predictive tool, not a measurement. Here is
        exactly how the number is produced.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Inputs.</strong> You provide age, sex, body weight, and
          height. Weight and height are read in either imperial (lb, ft-in) or
          metric (kg, cm) units, depending on your selection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unit conversion.</strong> Internally, values are stored in
          canonical metric units (kg and cm). When you switch unit systems,
          the calculator converts using
          <code>1 lb = 0.45359237 kg</code> and
          <code>1 in = 2.54 cm</code>, then keeps that canonical value in
          memory so that repeated toggles do not accumulate rounding error.
          If you edit a converted field manually, the canonical value updates
          from your new input.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Equation.</strong> The Mifflin-St Jeor or revised
          Harris-Benedict equation is applied using the exact canonical
          values, not the rounded display values.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rounding.</strong> The final result is rounded to the
          nearest whole kcal/day. Kilojoule output is derived from the rounded
          kcal value using <code>1 kcal = 4.184 kJ</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Limitations.</strong> The equations are population-level
          predictions. They do not directly measure your individual metabolic
          rate, and they do not adjust for body composition (lean vs fat
          mass), hormonal status, or medical conditions.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>How to Calculate BMR — Formula and Worked Example</h2>

      <p>
        BMR equations use body weight, height, age, and sex to predict resting
        energy expenditure. The Mifflin-St Jeor equation is widely considered
        the more accurate predictive formula for modern adults, and the
        Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics has endorsed it as the preferred
        equation for clinical use.
      </p>

      <div class="formula-box">
        <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor — Men</strong><br />
        <code>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) − 5 × age(y) + 5</code>
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor — Women</strong><br />
        <code>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) − 5 × age(y) − 161</code>
      </div>

      <div class="formula-box">
        <strong>Revised Harris-Benedict — Men</strong><br />
        <code>BMR = 13.397 × weight(kg) + 4.799 × height(cm) − 5.677 × age(y) + 88.362</code>
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Revised Harris-Benedict — Women</strong><br />
        <code>BMR = 9.247 × weight(kg) + 3.098 × height(cm) − 4.330 × age(y) + 447.593</code>
      </div>

      <h3>Worked Example (Mifflin-St Jeor)</h3>

      <p>
        Consider Mike, a 30-year-old male who weighs 175 lb (79.4 kg) and is
        5&prime;10&Prime; (177.8 cm) tall.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Convert to metric:</strong>
          <code>175 lb ÷ 2.2046 = 79.4 kg</code>;
          <code>5&prime;10&Prime; = 70 in = 177.8 cm</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Apply the Mifflin-St Jeor equation for men:</strong>
          <code>BMR = (10 × 79.4) + (6.25 × 177.8) − (5 × 30) + 5</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Solve step by step:</strong>
          <code>BMR = 794 + 1,111.25 − 150 + 5</code>
          <br />
          <code>BMR ≈ 1,760 kcal/day</code>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        The same inputs with the revised Harris-Benedict equation produce
        approximately <strong>1,830 kcal/day</strong>. The two equations can
        produce different estimates depending on individual inputs, which is
        why the Mifflin-St Jeor estimate is generally preferred for planning
        purposes.
      </p>

      <p class="calculator-note">
        The live calculator works with the exact values you enter, so your
        result may differ slightly from the rounded example above.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Typical BMR Ranges for Adults</h2>

      <p>
        The following reference values show typical BMR ranges for healthy
        non-athlete adults at common body weights. Individual BMR depends on
        body size, body composition, and other factors, so use these as
        reference points — not strict targets.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Typical adult BMR ranges by group and weight (Mifflin-St Jeor)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Group</th>
              <th scope="col">Example weight</th>
              <th scope="col">Typical BMR range</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Adult male, 20–40 years</td>
              <td>175 lb (79.4 kg)</td>
              <td>1,700–1,900 kcal/day</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Adult male, 40+ years</td>
              <td>175 lb (79.4 kg)</td>
              <td>1,550–1,750 kcal/day</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Adult female, 20–40 years</td>
              <td>140 lb (63.5 kg)</td>
              <td>1,350–1,500 kcal/day</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Adult female, 40+ years</td>
              <td>140 lb (63.5 kg)</td>
              <td>1,250–1,400 kcal/day</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="calculator-note">
        Athletes and very muscular individuals often have BMR values above
        these ranges. Values below the ranges are not automatically a medical
        issue, but persistent concerns are worth discussing with a qualified
        healthcare professional.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>BMR vs TDEE vs RMR — What Is the Difference?</h2>

      <p>
        BMR, RMR, and TDEE all describe energy expenditure, but at different
        levels of activity and measurement strictness.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>BMR, RMR, and TDEE at a glance</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Metric</th>
              <th scope="col">What it measures</th>
              <th scope="col">Typical share of TDEE</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate)</td>
              <td>Energy at complete rest under strict conditions</td>
              <td>~60–70%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>RMR (Resting Metabolic Rate)</td>
              <td>Energy at rest under relaxed conditions</td>
              <td>~60–75%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure)</td>
              <td>Total daily energy including activity and digestion</td>
              <td>100%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Predictive equations like Mifflin-St Jeor technically estimate RMR
        rather than strictly measured BMR, but the difference is small
        (usually under 10%) and the terms are often used interchangeably in
        consumer calculators.
      </p>

      <p>
        To estimate your total daily energy needs, use our
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/">TDEE Calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Factors That Affect Your BMR</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Body size and weight:</strong> Larger bodies generally
          require more resting energy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Height:</strong> Height is a variable in every predictive
          BMR equation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Age:</strong> Mifflin-St Jeor subtracts 5 kcal/day for each
          year of age, reflecting typical declines in lean mass and metabolic
          activity over time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sex:</strong> Predictive equations use different constants
          for men and women because of average differences in body composition.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Body composition:</strong> Lean tissue is more metabolically
          active than fat tissue, so muscle mass affects resting energy needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Individual variation:</strong> Genetics, hormones, and
          health conditions can shift measured BMR away from any predictive
          equation's output.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Because of this variation, two people with identical height, weight,
        age, and sex can still have different real-world resting energy needs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Using BMR as a Starting Point for Weight Loss</h2>

      <p>
        BMR by itself should not be used as a weight-loss calorie target.
        Eating below your BMR for extended periods may not meet your energy
        or nutrient needs and is generally not recommended without
        professional guidance.
      </p>

      <p>
        A more practical approach is to estimate your
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/tdee-calculator/">TDEE</a>
        first, then apply a moderate calorie deficit. Use the
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/calorie-deficit-calculator/">Calorie Deficit Calculator</a>
        to plan a daily target and projected timeline, and the
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/macro-calculator/">Macro Calculator</a>
        to split calories between protein, carbohydrate, and fat.
      </p>

      <p>
        Calculator results are estimates. Real-world weight trends over
        several weeks are the most useful feedback for adjusting your intake.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section sources">
      <h2>Sources &amp; Methodology</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Mifflin et al. (1990) — A new predictive equation for resting energy expenditure in healthy individuals, <em>Am J Clin Nutr</em>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6741850/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Roza &amp; Shizgal (1984) — The Harris-Benedict equation reevaluated, <em>Am J Clin Nutr</em>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10197591/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Frankenfield et al. (1998) — Comparison of predictive equations for resting metabolic rate, <em>J Am Diet Assoc</em>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278991/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            NIH StatPearls — Basal Metabolic Rate (physiology reference)
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{faq.question}</summary>
